Okay, the function is called `shouldReportBeInOptionList`.

The logic at line `6269` seems to be the one that hides empty chats:

```typescript
// Hide chats between two users that haven't been commented on from the LNH
if (excludeEmptyChats && isEmptyChat && isChatReport(report) && !isChatRoom(report) && !isPolicyExpenseChat(report) && !isSystemChat(report) && !isGroupChat(report) && canHideReport) {
    return false;
}
```

The problem is that this logic doesn't account for thread reports. It checks for `isChatReport`, but not `isChatThread`. Also, `isChatRoom` returns true for a thread inside a room, which is not what we want.

I need to add a condition to also hide empty *thread* reports. I'll modify the `if` condition to include `isChatThread(report)`.

Here's the plan:

1.  Add a check for `isChatThread(report)` to the `if` condition on line `6270`.
2.  The existing condition already checks for `isEmptyChat`, which should correctly identify if a thread has no comments.

Here is the modified code snippet:

```typescript
// Hide chats between two users that haven't been commented on from the LNH
if (excludeEmptyChats && isEmptyChat && (isChatReport(report) || isChatThread(report)) && !isChatRoom(report) && !isPolicyExpenseChat(report) && !isSystemChat(report) && !isGroupChat(report) && canHideReport) {
    return false;
}
```
This is not quite right. `isChatRoom` will be true for a thread in a room, and we want to hide it. The problem is that the existing logic is designed for one-on-one chats, not threads.

A better approach would be to add a new `if` block specifically for threads.

```typescript
// Hide empty thread reports
if (excludeEmptyChats && isEmptyChat && isChatThread(report)) {
    return false;
}
```

This is much simpler and more direct. It will hide any empty thread report, regardless of whether it's in a room or not. I will add this code after the existing check for empty chats.

Wait, `isChatThread` is already being checked at line `6186`. Let me look at the `isChatThread` function.

```typescript
function isChatThread(report: OnyxEntry<Report>): boolean {
    return report?.chatType === CONST.REPORT.CHAT_TYPE.CHAT && !!report.parentReportID && !!report.parentReportActionID;
}
```

This seems correct. Now, let's look at `isEmptyReport`.

```typescript
function isEmptyReport(report: OnyxEntry<Report>): boolean {
    const lastVisibleAction = ReportActionsUtils.getLastVisibleAction(report?.reportID ?? '-1');
    return !lastVisibleAction || ReportActionsUtils.isCreatedAction(lastVisibleAction);
}
```
This also seems correct. An empty report has no last visible action, or the last visible action is a "CREATED" action.

So, the problem is that the existing logic at `6270` is not correctly identifying thread reports that are in rooms.

I'll go with my second idea, which is to add a new `if` block specifically for threads. I'll add it right before the existing check for empty chats.

```typescript
// Hide empty thread reports
if (excludeEmptyChats && isEmptyChat && isChatThread(report)) {
    return false;
}

// Hide chats between two users that haven't been commented on from the LNH
if (excludeEmptyChats && isEmptyChat && isChatReport(report) && !isChatRoom(report) && !isPolicyExpenseChat(report) && !isSystemChat(report) && !isGroupChat(report) && canHideReport) {
    return false;
}
```

This should work. It will hide empty threads, and then the existing logic will hide empty one-on-one chats.

However, a thread report is also a `chatReport`. So the second `if` statement will also be true. Let me combine them.

The problem is that `isChatRoom(report)` is true for a thread in a room. I need to make sure that for threads, I don't check `isChatRoom`.

Here's a better condition:

```typescript
if (excludeEmptyChats && isEmptyChat) {
    if (isChatThread(report)) {
        return false;
    }
    if (isChatReport(report) && !isChatRoom(report) && !isPolicyExpenseChat(report) && !isSystemChat(report) && !isGroupChat(report) && canHideReport) {
        return false;
    }
